`readFile(file)` succeeded, then `res.writeHead` was called — no wait, the error is "Cannot write headers after they are sent", and the location is the `res.writeHead` at line 42 (catch → 404 path?). Line 42 is inside catch. So what happened: did `readFile` throw "after" the headers were sent? No — the order is: writeHead(200), then `res.end(await readFile(file))` — the await happens "before" res.end, but after writeHead has been called! If readFile throws (e.g. directory, or ENOENT), catch calls writeHead(404) → but the 200 headers have already been sent → ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT, an uncaught error crashes the node process.

Which file is failing? Probably `/favicon.ico` — wait, does the favicon request reach the server? The favicon request from earlier was probably a 404 (before the fix, everything was 404). Now: `/favicon.ico` → join(ROOT,'/favicon.ico') → doesn't exist → readFile throws "after" writeHead(200) → crash.

Fix: read the file before writeHead, and make catch safe:

```js
const server = createServer(async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const p = decodeURIComponent(new URL(req.url, 'http://x').pathname);
    const file = normalize(join(ROOT, p === '/' ? '/demo.html' : p));
    const rootNorm = normalize(ROOT);
    if (file !== rootNorm && !file.startsWith(rootNorm + sep)) throw new Error('traversal');
    const data = await readFile(file);
    res.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': MIME[extname(file)] || 'application/octet-stream' });
    res.end(data);
  } catch {
    if (!res.headersSent) {
      res.writeHead(404);
      res.end('not found');
    } else {
      res.destroy();
    }
  }
});
```

